      "In the ancient city of London, on a certain autumn day in the second quarter of the sixteenth century, a boy was born to a poor family of the name of Canty, who did not want him. On the same day another English child was born to a rich family of the name of Tudor, who did want him. All England wanted him too. England had so longed for him, and hoped for him, and prayed God for him, that, now that he was really come, the people went nearly mad for joy.",
      "Mere acquaintances hugged and kissed each other and cried. Everybody took a holiday, and high and low, rich and poor, feasted and danced and sang, and got very mellow; and they kept this up for days and nights. By day, London was a sight to see, with gay banners waving from every balcony and housetop, and splendid pageants marching along. By night, it was another sight to see, with its great bonfires at every corner, and its crowds of merrymakers around them. There was no talk in all England but of the new baby, Edward Tudor, Prince of Wales, who lay in silken swaddling clothes, dreaming of nothing, and knowing nothing of all this fuss.",
      "There was no talk about the other baby, Tom Canty, who lay in his rags of clothes, dreaming of nothing either. Only the family of beggars whom he had come to trouble made any fuss, and they made it in curses.",
      "A few years passed. London was fifteen hundred years old, and was a great town—for that day. It had a hundred thousand inhabitants—some think double as many. The streets were very narrow, and crooked, and dirty, especially in the part where Tom Canty lived, which was not far from London Bridge. The houses were of wood, with the second story projecting over the first, and the third projecting over the second, so that the opposite roofs leaned towards each other and nearly touched, shutting out the sun and sky.",
      "The house which Tom's father lived in was in a foul little court called Offal Court, out of Pudding Lane. It was small, decayed, and rickety, but it was packed full of wretchedly poor families. Canty's tribe occupied a room on the third floor. The mother and father had a sort of bedstead in the corner; but Tom, his grandmother, and his two sisters, Bet and Nan, were not restricted to any particular spot; they slept on the floor, and had what was left of blankets to cover themselves with.",
      "In the ancient city of London, on a certain autumn day in the second quarter of the sixteenth century, a boy was born to a poor family of the name of Canty, who did not want him. On the same day another English child was born to a rich family of the name of Tudor, who did want him. All England, in fact, had wanted him, had hoped for him, and had prayed God for him, and now that he was really come, the people went nearly mad for joy.",
      "Mere acquaintances hugged and kissed each other and cried. Everybody made a holiday, and high and low, rich and poor, feasted and danced and sang, and got very mellow; and they kept this up for days and nights. There was no talk in all England but of the new baby, Edward Tudor, Prince of Wales, who lay in silks and satins, unconscious of all this fuss, and not knowing that great lords and ladies were watching over him. But there was no talk about the other baby, Tom Canty, wrapped in his poor rags, except among the few half-starved paupers whom he had just come to trouble with his presence.",
      "London was fifteen hundred years old, and was a great town for that day. The streets were very narrow, and crooked, and dirty, especially in the part where Tom Canty lived, which was not far from London Bridge. It was called Offal Court. It was a dreadful, filthy place, full of drunken brawls and squalor. Yet young Tom dreamed of palaces and kings, reading every scrap of old books he could beg from good Father Andrew.",
      "One glorious day, wandering far from Offal Court, Tom found himself before the magnificent gilded gates of Westminster Palace. Peering through the golden bars, he saw a handsome lad dressed in shimmering satin and sparkling jewels, surrounded by bowing guards. It was Edward, the Prince of Wales! When the palace guards struck Tom for daring to look, the young Prince sprang forward in righteous anger: 'How dare you treat a poor subject so! Open the gates and let the lad in!'",
      "Inside the royal apartment, the two boys marveled at their astonishing resemblance. Laughing with delight, they decided to exchange clothes for a merry jest. But when Tom Canty stood dressed in royal velvet and Prince Edward in ragged fustian, they looked into the great mirror: they were identical in every feature! When the young Prince rushed to the gates to rebuke the guard, the soldier cuffed his ear and cast him into the streets as a filthy beggar, launching the prince into the bitter underworld of England, while Tom the pauper was left upon the throne to rule an empire.",
      "On the day of the coronation at Westminster Abbey, Tom Canty stepped down from the throne and placed the crown upon the head of the rightful king, Edward VI. King Edward rewarded Tom with high honors and made him the protector of Christ's Hospital, never forgetting the lessons of mercy and justice he had learned while wandering as a pauper through the humble lanes of England.",
      "The enduring power of The Prince and the Pauper lies in its vivid depiction of human nature, showing how adventures, challenges, and unexpected fortunes test the character and resolve of ordinary people. Through every trial, the characters discover that resilience and honor are their greatest assets.",
      "Whether traversing vast oceans, wandering across wild countrysides, or confronting moral dilemmas in bustling towns, the narrative of Mark Twain demonstrates that genuine wisdom is forged in experience. Truth and loyalty invariably outlast deception and greed.",
      "Looking back upon the rich tapestry of events in The Prince and the Pauper, we perceive that every difficulty encountered on the journey was a stepping-stone toward maturity, understanding, and lasting peace.",
      "Through every page of The Prince and the Pauper, Mark Twain crafts a timeless testament to the enduring beauty of literature, inviting every reader to reflect deeply upon life, language, and the noble quest for understanding.",
