      "While young Tom Canty reigned in the royal apartments of Westminster Palace—learning to sign state decrees, abolish cruel executions, and govern with natural compassion—the true Prince of Wales, Edward Tudor, was wandering the squalid streets of London in beggar's rags. Beaten by Tom's brutal father, John Canty, the prince was rescued by Miles Hendon, a brave and chivalrous soldier of fortune who took pity on the ragged lad, believing him to be deranged when he claimed to be the King of England.",
      "Miles Hendon wrapped the prince in his own cloak, shared his meager food, and humorously humored his royal pretensions, asking as his sole reward the hereditary privilege of sitting down in the presence of the King of England! Together, they traveled across the English countryside, witnessing firsthand the cruel injustices of the laws: poor cottagers branded with hot irons for begging, women burned at the stake for petty witchcraft, and farmers driven into slavery for debt.",
      "These bitter trials burned deep into young Edward's soul. 'When I am king,' he vowed through his tears, 'the laws shall be gentle, and mercy shall temper justice!' On the morning of the coronation, just as the Archbishop of Canterbury was about to place the crown upon Tom Canty's head, the ragged Prince of Wales marched up the aisle of the Abbey, confronting Tom face to face. With noble honesty, Tom knelt before the true king, helping him prove his identity by retrieving the Great Seal of England, ensuring that the boy who had known the suffering of the poor would reign as one of the wisest, most merciful monarchs in English history.",
